  "live_records": {
    "q-94495": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94495",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2})$ with $P(X>2)=0.2981,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.695\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =1-P(X>2) \\\\  & =1-0.2981 \\\\  & =0.7019\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{2-\\mu }{5}) & =0.7019 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{2-\\mu }{5}) & =0.7019 \\\\ \\frac{2-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7019)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.53)=0.7019\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7019)=0.53.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-\\mu }{5} & =0.53 \\\\ 2-\\mu  & =2.65 \\\\ \\mu  & =-0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
