    "q-94831": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94831",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{2.5}^{2})$ with $P(X<-1.5)=0.0526,$ find $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0548\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0668\\,$ | $\\,0.0655\\,$ | $\\,0.0643\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-1.5)=0.0526\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le -1.5)=0.0526\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -1.5) & =0.0526 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{-1.5-\\mu }{2.5}) & =0.0526 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{-1.5-\\mu }{2.5}) & =0.0526 \\\\ \\frac{-1.5-\\mu }{2.5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.0526)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.62)=0.0526\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.0526)=-1.62.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-1.5-\\mu }{2.5} & =-1.62 \\\\ -1.5-\\mu  & =-4.05 \\\\ \\mu  & =2.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
