    "q-342033": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342033",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(|X|<3.2)=0.7330,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<3.2)=P(-3.2<X<3.2).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3.2<X<3.2) & =0.7330 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3.2) & =0.7330 \\\\ P(0<X<3.2) & =0.3665 \\\\ P(X<3.2)-P(X<0) & =0.3665 \\\\ P(X<3.2)-0.5 & =0.3665 \\\\ P(X<3.2) & =0.8665 \\\\ P(X\\le 3.2) & =0.8665\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-coring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3.2) & =0.8665 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3.2-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.8665 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3.2}{\\sigma }) & =0.8665 \\\\ \\frac{3.2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8665)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.11)=0.8665\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8665)=1.11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3.2}{\\sigma } & =1.11 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.2}{1.11} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.882\\,88.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(2.882\\,88)^{2} \\\\  & ≈8.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
