    "q-210712": {
      "dom_id": "step-q210712",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(|X|<5)=0.8198,$ find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(|X|<5)=P(-5<X<5).\n$$\n\nThen, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-5<X<5) & =0.8198 \\\\ 2P(0<X<5) & =0.8198 \\\\ P(0<X<5) & =0.4099 \\\\ P(X<5)-P(X<0) & =0.4099 \\\\ P(X<5)-0.5 & =0.4099 \\\\ P(X<5) & =0.9099 \\\\ P(X\\le 5) & =0.9099\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =0.9099 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{5-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.9099 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.9099 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9099)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.34)=0.9099\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9099)=1.34.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =1.34 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{5}{1.34} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3.73.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
