    "q-132312": {
      "dom_id": "step-q132312",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-4,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-18<X<10)=0.1113,$ find  $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5080\\,$ | $\\,0.5120\\,$ | $\\,0.5160\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-18<X<10$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =-4.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the given interval is symmetric about the mean:\n\n$$\nP(-18<X<10)=P(-4-14<X<-4+14)\n$$\n\nSo, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-18<X<10) & =0.1113 \\\\ 2P(-4<X<10) & =0.1113 \\\\ P(-4<X<10) & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10)-P(X<-4) & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10)-0.5 & =0.0557 \\\\ P(X<10) & =0.5557 \\\\ P(X\\le 10) & =0.5557\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10) & =0.5557 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{10-(-4)}{\\sigma }) & =0.5557 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{14}{\\sigma }) & =0.5557 \\\\ \\frac{14}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5557)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.14)=0.5557\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5557)=0.14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14}{\\sigma } & =0.14 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{14}{0.14} \\\\ \\sigma  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
