    "q-342071": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342071",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(5.6,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(1.2<X<10.0)=0.9722,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.9861\\,$ | $\\,0.9864\\,$ | $\\,0.9868\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9893\\,$ | $\\,0.9896\\,$ | $\\,0.9898\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $1.2<X<10.0$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =5.6.$*\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.2<X<10.0) & =0.9722 \\\\ 2P(5.6<X<10.0) & =0.9722 \\\\ P(5.6<X<10.0) & =0.4861 \\\\ P(X<10.0)-P(X<5.6) & =0.4861 \\\\ P(X<10.0)-0.5 & =0.4861 \\\\ P(X<10.0) & =0.9861 \\\\ P(X\\le 10.0) & =0.9861\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10.0) & =0.9861 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{10.0-5.6}{\\sigma }) & =0.9861 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4.4}{\\sigma }) & =0.9861 \\\\ \\frac{4.4}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9861)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(2.2)=0.9861\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9861)=2.2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4}{\\sigma } & =2.2 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4.4}{2.2} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
